From f21725d4c191d03d988cee5348c07c7e365ee79b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: samhenrigold <49251320+samhenrigold@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 22:49:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] TIGER: make the IPC wire format identical in a 32-bit and a
 64-bit process

Prepares the message set for the split where a 32-bit UI process talks to a
64-bit content process.

The socket framing header, IPC::MessageInfo, was three size_t fields and a bool
blitted raw onto the socket: 12 bytes on i386 and 24 on x86_64, which would
desynchronise the stream at the first message. It is now fixed-width with
explicit padding and static_asserts on size, alignment and every offset. The
new struct is 12 bytes on both, so the 32-bit side's framing is unchanged.

Two message fields were size_t and are now uint32_t: SnapOffset::snapAreaIndices
indexes the snap areas on a page and PlatformXR::LayerInfo::numImages counts
swapchain images, so neither is near 2^32, and every consumer already widens
them.

The arithmetic argument coder now rejects long, unsigned long and long double,
which covers size_t, ptrdiff_t and uintptr_t as they are spelled on Darwin, so a
future field of one of those types fails to compile instead of corrupting
messages. int64_t and uint64_t are long long on Darwin and are unaffected.

Alignment is on the wire too, which an audit of field types alone does not
catch. Encoder::encodeSpan pads the stream to alignof(T) and Decoder::decodeSpan
seeks to the same boundary, and alignof is 4 for uint64_t, int64_t and double on
Darwin i386 against 8 on x86_64. A message of { uint32_t, uint64_t, uint32_t,
double } lays out at 0, 4, 12, 16 for 24 bytes on i386 and at 0, 8, 16, 24 for
32 bytes on x86_64. Nothing has to be declared wrongly for that: a uint64_t is
enough, and uint64_t is what every container length and every ObjectIdentifier
already uses. Both sides now pad by wireAlignmentOf, which gives 8-byte scalars
an alignment of 8 everywhere.

Element size is on the wire as well. ArgumentCoder<std::span<T>> bulk-copies
size * sizeof(T), and wireAlignmentOf fixes where a field starts, not how wide
it is. A composite element with an 8-byte member is laid out differently by the
two ABIs: { uint32_t; uint64_t; } measures 12 bytes on i386 and 16 on x86_64, so
a span of a hundred of them disagrees by 400 bytes. The span coder now requires
isWireStableSpanElement, which admits scalars and enums and otherwise demands
that nothing in the element needs 8-byte alignment. Only the x86_64 build can
evaluate that, since i386 reports alignof 4 for such a struct either way, so
both builds have to run.

Proven on the box by spike/ipcabi.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RHwCbDKQLvzXACuucjKev6
